  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nFog potential tonight...\n\nSome patchy fog may develop across eastern Wisconsin overnight\nwith light winds and mostly clear skies. Models hint that fog\ndevelopment would not be as ubiquitous or as dense as this \nmorning. \n\nShower and storm chances (15-30%) return late tonight through \nWednesday...\n\nShowers and storms developing this evening over the Dakotas \nforecast to produce shortwave/upper jet that shifts east toward \nWisconsin late tonight. Gradient of instability from MN to \nsouthern WI, as is the stronger convergence with LLJ. Even so, \nwith the wave and elevated instability farther north, cannot rule \nout some showers and storms on Tuesday morning, with perhaps \nslight boost in coverage farther north during peak heating of \nafternoon as MLCAPEs increase over 1000J/kg. Effective shear over \n40 kts would support stronger storm if convection develops. SPC \ndoes keep the marginal risk for severe farther south near the more\npersistent frontal boundary/highest instability. \n\nRain chances (30-50%) continues Tuesday night into Wednesday as \nanother shortwave moves through the region and the front remains \nclose enough to our area. Similar to expectations for late tonight\nand Tuesday, appears better signal for severe remains south. \nHowever, late Tuesday night through Wednesday, there is stronger \nforcing farther north and sufficient instability. Seems like this \ntime frame may hold the greatest chances for some stronger storms \nover our area, especially if things slow a bit and these factors \ncatch up to at least the beginning of peak heating on Wednesday.\nMachine learning progs show a weak \"bump\" in severe probs to \n5-15% for Wednesday after a relative minimum in severe potential\nearlier in the week.\n\nDry and cooler conditions are then expected late this week, then \nchances for rain (30-60%) return for the weekend...\n\nHigh pressure builds in across the area Wednesday night through \nFriday, pushing the front and lingering instability south and \ngenerally keeping the area dry through the rest of the week. Highs\nwill settle into the 70s regionwide. The high eventually shifts \neast, allowing for the chance for showers and thunderstorms to \nreturn to the area Friday night into the weekend. Looking at the \nentire weekend it appears Sunday would be the best chance out of \nthe two days to be dry.",
